1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,203
Five classmates and four
blood groups: A, B, AB and O.

2
00:00:04,203 --> 00:00:08,041
Pair every student with every
group, and you get twenty pairs.

3
00:00:08,441 --> 00:00:10,998
But only a few of those pairs are true.

4
00:00:10,998 --> 00:00:13,351
Ravi has one blood group, not four.

5
00:00:13,351 --> 00:00:15,955
And Joel has not even done his test yet.

6
00:00:16,405 --> 00:00:19,616
Diya is making the list
for a blood donation camp.

7
00:00:19,966 --> 00:00:23,671
How do we keep just the true
pairs, and describe them in maths?

8
00:00:24,171 --> 00:00:26,780
We pick them out of the Cartesian product.

9
00:00:26,780 --> 00:00:28,965
The pairs we keep form a relation.

10
00:00:29,615 --> 00:00:32,103
Start with the full Cartesian product.

11
00:00:32,103 --> 00:00:34,254
S is the set of five students.

12
00:00:34,254 --> 00:00:36,662
G is the set of four blood groups.

13
00:00:37,012 --> 00:00:40,151
S cross G has five times
four, twenty pairs.

14
00:00:40,151 --> 00:00:42,345
Every possible link is in here.

15
00:00:42,745 --> 00:00:46,925
Now test each pair with one rule:
student x has blood group y.

16
00:00:46,925 --> 00:00:49,317
Pairs that fail the rule fade away.

17
00:00:49,717 --> 00:00:51,193
Four pairs survive.

18
00:00:51,193 --> 00:00:54,957
This chosen set of pairs
is a relation from S to G.

19
00:00:55,607 --> 00:01:00,958
In NCERT words: a relation R from a
non empty set A to a non empty set

20
00:01:00,958 --> 00:01:04,809
B is a subset of the
Cartesian product A cross B.

21
00:01:05,209 --> 00:01:09,252
The subset is picked by a relationship
between the first element and the

22
00:01:09,252 --> 00:01:11,032
second element of each pair.

23
00:01:11,432 --> 00:01:15,850
If x comma y is in R, then
y is called the image of x.

24
00:01:15,850 --> 00:01:17,349
Ravi's image is O.

25
00:01:17,999 --> 00:01:20,378
Every relation comes with three sets.

26
00:01:20,378 --> 00:01:21,615
Watch the arrows.

27
00:01:21,965 --> 00:01:26,085
The domain is the set of all
first elements of the pairs in R.

28
00:01:26,085 --> 00:01:28,760
Only students with an arrow leaving them.

29
00:01:28,760 --> 00:01:32,015
Joel has no arrow, so
he is not in the domain.

30
00:01:32,765 --> 00:01:35,637
The range is the set
of all second elements.

31
00:01:35,637 --> 00:01:38,913
Only the groups an arrow
lands on: O, A and B.

32
00:01:39,663 --> 00:01:42,467
The codomain is the whole second set, G.

33
00:01:42,467 --> 00:01:45,985
All four groups, even AB,
which no arrow reached.

34
00:01:46,735 --> 00:01:47,835
Side by side.

35
00:01:47,835 --> 00:01:50,717
The range is what the arrows actually hit.

36
00:01:50,717 --> 00:01:52,224
Here, three groups.

37
00:01:52,624 --> 00:01:55,334
The codomain is everything they could hit.

38
00:01:55,334 --> 00:01:56,733
Here, four groups.

39
00:01:56,733 --> 00:01:59,649
So the range always sits
inside the codomain.

40
00:02:00,249 --> 00:02:01,276
Now you try.

41
00:02:01,276 --> 00:02:04,364
Joel's test comes back,
and he is group AB.

42
00:02:04,364 --> 00:02:05,949
Does the range change?

43
00:02:05,949 --> 00:02:07,174
Pause and think.

44
00:02:10,374 --> 00:02:11,274
Yes!

45
00:02:10,914 --> 00:02:15,626
A new arrow lands on AB, so the
range grows to all four groups.

46
00:02:15,626 --> 00:02:17,743
The codomain does not change.

47
00:02:17,743 --> 00:02:19,109
It was always G.

48
00:02:19,759 --> 00:02:20,825
Remember this.

49
00:02:20,825 --> 00:02:23,348
A relation is a chosen set of pairs.

50
00:02:23,348 --> 00:02:25,700
Its first elements make the domain.

51
00:02:25,700 --> 00:02:28,053
Its second elements make the range.

52
00:02:28,753 --> 00:02:32,071
Now, how many different relations
can we make from A to B?

53
00:02:32,421 --> 00:02:33,321
Step one.

54
00:02:33,304 --> 00:02:36,200
A relation is any subset of A cross B.

55
00:02:36,950 --> 00:02:37,890
Step two.

56
00:02:37,890 --> 00:02:43,654
If A has p elements and B has q
elements, A cross B has p times q pairs.

57
00:02:44,404 --> 00:02:45,304
Step three.

58
00:02:45,262 --> 00:02:48,721
Each pair has two choices:
keep it, or leave it out.

59
00:02:49,471 --> 00:02:50,371
Step four.

60
00:02:50,355 --> 00:02:55,223
Two choices for every pair means two
times two times two, once per pair.

61
00:02:55,223 --> 00:02:57,439
That is two to the power p q.

62
00:02:58,189 --> 00:03:00,491
Watch it with two times two pairs.

63
00:03:00,491 --> 00:03:04,978
Four switches, sixteen different
relations, from no pairs at all to every

64
00:03:04,978 --> 00:03:05,878
pair.

65
00:03:06,171 --> 00:03:07,906
Now, one worked example.

66
00:03:08,256 --> 00:03:09,670
A is one to six.

67
00:03:09,670 --> 00:03:13,083
R is the relation on A,
where y equals two x.

68
00:03:13,083 --> 00:03:17,710
Write R in roster form, then find
its domain, range and codomain.

69
00:03:18,210 --> 00:03:19,110
Step one.

70
00:03:19,032 --> 00:03:20,065
Test each x.

71
00:03:20,065 --> 00:03:24,828
Two times one is two, two times two
is four, two times three is six.

72
00:03:24,828 --> 00:03:27,937
Two times four is eight,
which is not in A.

73
00:03:28,687 --> 00:03:29,587
Step two.

74
00:03:29,520 --> 00:03:33,763
So R is one comma two, two
comma four, and three comma six.

75
00:03:34,513 --> 00:03:35,413
Step three.

76
00:03:35,377 --> 00:03:38,853
The domain is the first
elements: one, two and three.

77
00:03:39,603 --> 00:03:40,503
Step four.

78
00:03:40,447 --> 00:03:43,992
The range is the second
elements: two, four and six.

79
00:03:44,742 --> 00:03:45,642
Step five.

80
00:03:45,596 --> 00:03:48,285
The codomain is all of A, one to six.

81
00:03:48,285 --> 00:03:51,887
A relation from A to itself
is called a relation on A.

82
00:03:52,537 --> 00:03:54,003
Now, the exam trap.

83
00:03:54,353 --> 00:03:56,728
A has three elements and B has two.

84
00:03:56,728 --> 00:04:00,961
Many students count the relations as
two cubed times two squared, which

85
00:04:00,961 --> 00:04:02,043
is thirty two.

86
00:04:02,443 --> 00:04:06,325
But relations are subsets of A
cross B, which has six pairs.

87
00:04:06,325 --> 00:04:09,626
So the count is two to
the power six, sixty four.

88
00:04:10,276 --> 00:04:11,815
Where is this asked?

89
00:04:11,815 --> 00:04:15,109
In PUC exams, as: find
the domain and range.

90
00:04:15,109 --> 00:04:20,211
In JEE Main, as: count the pairs in
a relation, or count the relations.

91
00:04:20,811 --> 00:04:22,060
Exam level now.

92
00:04:22,060 --> 00:04:24,250
A is the numbers one to ten.

93
00:04:24,250 --> 00:04:29,330
R is the set of pairs x comma y in
A cross A with x plus two y equal to

94
00:04:29,330 --> 00:04:30,230
twelve.

95
00:04:30,028 --> 00:04:34,621
How many elements are in the
domain of R, union the range of R?

96
00:04:34,621 --> 00:04:35,923
Pause and solve.

97
00:04:41,323 --> 00:04:42,486
The answer is B.

98
00:04:42,486 --> 00:04:43,386
Eight.

99
00:04:43,535 --> 00:04:45,528
x equals twelve minus two y.

100
00:04:45,528 --> 00:04:49,931
For y from one to five, x is
ten, eight, six, four and two.

101
00:04:50,431 --> 00:04:54,331
y equals six would make x
zero, which is not in A.

102
00:04:54,331 --> 00:04:56,024
So R has five pairs.

103
00:04:56,524 --> 00:04:59,469
The domain is two, four, six, eight, ten.

104
00:04:59,469 --> 00:05:01,192
The range is one to five.

105
00:05:01,192 --> 00:05:02,813
They share two and four.

106
00:05:03,313 --> 00:05:07,562
So the union has five plus five
minus two, which is eight elements.

107
00:05:08,162 --> 00:05:09,621
Here is your recap.

108
00:05:09,621 --> 00:05:11,120
Screenshot this card.

109
00:05:14,320 --> 00:05:17,157
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

110
00:05:17,557 --> 00:05:18,457
One.

111
00:05:18,088 --> 00:05:22,971
For R equal to one comma three, two
comma five, two comma seven, write

112
00:05:22,971 --> 00:05:24,846
the domain and the range.

113
00:05:25,746 --> 00:05:26,646
Two.

114
00:05:26,201 --> 00:05:30,364
How many relations are there from a
set of two elements to a set of three

115
00:05:30,364 --> 00:05:31,264
elements?

116
00:05:31,942 --> 00:05:32,842
Three.

117
00:05:32,581 --> 00:05:35,650
Why is the range always
inside the codomain?

118
00:05:36,750 --> 00:05:40,596
Next time: every roll number
points to exactly one student.

119
00:05:40,596 --> 00:05:45,251
When every element gets exactly one
arrow, a relation becomes a function.
